    "created_at": "2026-03-11T12:06:58Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-03-11T12:06:58Z",
    "body": "I'm not terribly enthusiastic about a union type for simple aesthetics (`typedef (ExtendableMessageEvent or HTMLHyperlinkElementUtils or Location or ...) OriginExtractableObject;` is pretty verbose and brittle). Not calling `toString()` is certainly a web-visible implication of the way we've defined things, but it seems pretty reasonable to me that we'd not even try to extract an origin from an object that isn't defined as having an origin representation.\n\nThat said, I don't have strong opinions at all about the way we define and implement this. In Chromium, I did most of the work through multiple inheritance with a class defining the internal representation of the \"extract an origin\" method ([`DOMOriginUtils`](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/url/dom_origin_utils.h;l=15)), and ended up casting from all the listed types anyway. If there was a similar construct in IDL that would allow us to define a group of objects that could be expected to implement a given method, using it would be fine by me.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
